WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.920
In 1817, the critic William
Hazlitt made a bold claim:

2
00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.520
"It is WE who are Hamlet."

3
00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:11.180
As the dates run on, the
figure keeps changing shape.

4
00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.550
A thinker, hunched over a book.

5
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.020
A grieving son.

6
00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.020
A patient on a couch.

7
00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.360
A man watched by eyes behind curtains.

8
00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:24.730
Then the light leaves him, and
finds a woman holding flowers.

9
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.030
Every age finds its own Hamlet.

10
00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.620
So whose Hamlet is the right one?

11
00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.510
In this mission, you'll
meet the Romantic Hamlet,

12
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.680
a mind full of thought
and short of action.

13
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.400
You'll weigh Bradley's tragic
hero, then psychoanalytic,

14
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.990
formalist and political readings.

15
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:46.280
And you'll see what feminist and
historicist critics ask instead.

16
00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:49.500
Your mission: decide
which reading is right.
